esses. Unexpected modifications or writes to this section can indicate tampering or code injection. [Data: Entities (339)]\\n\\nThe record also describes module stomping as an example in which an unused legitimate DLL's .text section is overwritten with shellcode, although no specific incident or actor is associated with the community. This makes the text section a relevant inspection target within the broader memory-analysis model. [Data: Entities (339)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Copy-on-write behavior can reveal modified shared pages\",\"explanation\":\"COPY-ON-WRITE is linked to the TEXT SECTION and MEMORY PAGE. The relationships state that read-only .text sections should normally be shared, so Copy-On-Write activity can reveal unusual modification, while page-level write-state checks can indicate whether a memory page was modified after being shared. [Data: Entities (322, 354); Relationships (368, 367)]\\n\\nThese relationships describe a supporting diagnostic mechanism for identifying changes to memory that would ordinarily remain shared or read-only. The evidence does not establish that every Copy-On-Write event is malicious or that the mechanism alone confirms code injection. [Data: Relationships (367, 368)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Pattern matching is represented through GREP\",\"explanation\":\"GREP is included as a text-search utility and is linked to MEMORY SIGNATURE SCANNING as a simple pattern-search analogy. [Data: Entities (338); Relationships (366)]\\n\\nThis supports the interpretation that the community uses pattern matching as a conceptual model for comparing memory contents with known signatures. The records do not state that GREP itself is deployed as an antivirus engine or that it performs the full memory-scanning function. [Data: Relationships (366); Entities (242, 338)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community has high cybersecurity relevance but limited contextual evidence\",\"explanation\":\"The entities and relationships collectively describe malware detection, p